<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Czech Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Czechia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Czech Republic, or Czechia, is a landlocked country in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> made up of the historical lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> as its capital. It became independent on 1 January 1993 after the peaceful dissolution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. It joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1999 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/czech-republic.jpg" alt="Czech Republic" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaroslav Kursa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Czech_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Czechoslovakia ceased to exist on 31 December 1992, succeeded on 1 January 1993 by the <b>Czech Republic</b> and Slovakia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Great Moravia was the first major state that was predominantly West Slavic, centred on the Morava River, which gave it its name, in today's eastern <b>Czech Republic</b> and western Slovakia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century the historic rights of the Bohemian Crown became the basis of Czech national claims, articulated by the historian František Palacký and the Czech National Revival, and the historic frontiers of Bohemia, Moravia and Czech Silesia still largely form the borders of the <b>Czech Republic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Moravia survives as a historical land forming the eastern part of the <b>Czech Republic</b>, with Brno as its largest city and a strong regional identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It is generally held to have been standardised by two Byzantine missionaries from Thessaloniki, Cyril and his brother Methodius, for a mission to Great Moravia, roughly today’s eastern <b>Czech Republic</b> and western Slovakia, sent in answer to a request by its ruler, Duke Rastislav, for a Slavonic liturgy.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Ethnic tensions between Czechs and Slovaks resurfaced after communism, and disputes over national governance led to the peaceful division of Czechoslovakia into the <b>Czech Republic</b> and the Slovak Republic on 31 December 1992.</blockquote><a href="/movement/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
